' ======================================================================
' L3 — transport / bdx: self-contained protocol status
' ======================================================================

package "L3  rs-matter-transport  /  rs-matter-bdx" #FFF7ED {

  enum SCStatusCodes <<spec, proto/sc.rs>> {
    6 values
    + as_report() : StatusReport
  }
  enum GeneralCode <<spec>> {
    18 values
  }
  class StatusReport <<spec — the envelope>> {
    + general_code : GeneralCode
    + proto_id   : u32
    + proto_code : u16
    + proto_data : &[u8]
  }
  enum BdxStatus <<spec, repr(u16)>> {
    14 values
    + as_report() : StatusReport
  }

  StatusReport *-- GeneralCode
  SCStatusCodes --> StatusReport : as_report()
  BdxStatus     --> StatusReport : as_report()
}

note bottom of StatusReport
  **Out of scope, and genuinely so.**
  ZERO conversions to or from Error anywhere
  in the workspace. These converge on
  StatusReport, not on StatusIB.

  Also the existing precedent for layered
  status: a generic outer code plus a
  protocol-specific inner code, spec-mandated
  and already working.

  Adding them to ErrorCode would cost 1 -> 2
  bytes (a 2nd payload variant kills niche
  folding), or 1 -> 4 with BdxStatus's
  repr(u16). Measured. Declined.
end note

' ======================================================================
' L4 — rs-matter-dm: cluster handlers and hook errors
' ======================================================================

package "L4  rs-matter-dm  (<- im, node, sc, transport, bdx, dm-clusters, etc)" #F8FAFF {

  enum NetCtlError <<net_comm.rs>> {
    NetworkNotFound
    UnsupportedSecurity, AuthFailure
    OtherConnectionFailure
    IpBindFailed, IpV6Failed
    --
    Other(Error)
  }

  enum "WebRtcError / PushAvError /\nZoneError / CamAvError /\nCamAvSettingsError" as Hooks <<5 types, app/star.rs>> {
    IMStatusCode subsets,
    hand-rolled 5x
    ..
    **no fault channel at all** --
    an implementor whose SPI dies
    must return ::Failure
    ..
    each has From<X> for Error
  }

  enum CommonStatusCode <<mode.rs, ranged u8>> {
    common  0x00..=0x3F
    derived 0x40..=0x7F
    mfg     0x80..=0xBF
  }
  class ModeChangeError <<mode.rs, opaque>> {
    - status : u8
    - text : &'static str
  }
}

Error --> NetCtlError : From -> Other
NetCtlError *-- Error
Hooks -[#B91C1C]-> Error : **From**, via ErrorCode\nall 5 target only ~7 codes,\nevery one status-like

note bottom of Hooks
  The **cross-cutting** case: subsets of the
  GLOBAL IMStatusCode, not of any cluster
  enum. Only PushAVStreamTransport has real
  spec-defined cluster codes (12, currently
  unreachable from a hook).

  zone_management's generated StatusCodeEnum
  is an **errata artifact**, not spec --
  do NOT wire it up.

  CommonStatusCode + ModeChangeError are why
  a closed enum over all cluster codes cannot
  work: one is a RANGED u8 with derived/mfg
  namespaces, the other carries mandatory
  status text.
end note

' ======================================================================
' The blocked edge
' ======================================================================

ErrorCode -[#B91C1C,dashed]-> IMStatusCode : **BLOCKED**\nErrorCode::Status(IMStatusCode)\nL0 cannot name L1

note as N_SIZE
  **Measured (rustc -O, size_of)**

  ErrorCode flat, today            1
  + Status(IMStatusCode)           1   <- free, ONE payload variant
  + Status(Status) w/ Option<u16>  6
  + Status(Status) w/ NonZeroU8    2
  + Status + Sc                    2   <- 2nd payload variant
  + Status + Sc + Bdx (repr u16)   4

  Niche folding works for EXACTLY ONE
  payload-carrying variant. A second forces
  an explicit discriminant.

  **Consequence for per-crate collation:**
  a root error with one variant per internal
  crate is ~10 payload variants, so folding
  does not apply and the root error grows.
  Paid on Result<(),Error> -- 55% of all
  Result returns pre-split.
end note

note as N_RULES
  **Rules that survive the split**

  1. Widening (lossless) gets `From`.
     Narrowing gets a NAME. Never both --
     `?` will find the lossy one.

  2. Nesting has a direction, now enforced by
     Cargo as well as by the type system:
     - nested BELOW Error: plain data, Copy,
       no Error inside, the ONLY payload
       variant, and in Error's crate or lower
     - WRAPPING Error: carries Other/Fault(Error),
       lives above it
     - nothing can be both

  3. A collating error sits ABOVE what it
     collates; a shared vocabulary error sits
     BELOW its users. Two types, two jobs.
     rs-matter-utils can only host the second.
end note

N_SIZE -[hidden]- N_RULES

@enduml
